{
  "id": "phase-035",
  "title": "Remove the reachable panics in the time builtins",
  "severity": "major",
  "depends_on": [],
  "risk": "low",
  "finding": "src/vm.rs:924, src/vm.rs:1089, src/vm.rs:1238, src/vm.rs:1250 and src/vm.rs:1263 each call `SystemTime::now().duration_since(UNIX_EPOCH).unwrap()`. `duration_since` returns `Err(SystemTimeError)` for any instant before 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z and `.unwrap()` on it panics. All five sit inside the time builtins, reachable from a plain Redblue program through `time.now()` with no test, no flag and no unusual input — only a host clock set before the epoch. Every other error path in those functions is a RuntimeError; these five unwind through the interpreter thread and abort the process. This is the reachable-`unwrap` class the reviewer contract (AGENTS.md section 5) names as a hunt target, and it is the only place in src/ where `.unwrap()` appears outside test code — the other 7 unwraps are all in `mod tests` blocks.",
  "goal": "Turn the five clock unwraps into RuntimeErrors, so no Redblue program can panic the interpreter through a host clock set before the Unix epoch.",
  "must_touch": [
    "src/"
  ],
  "accept": [
    "no `.unwrap()` remains in src/ outside a `#[cfg(test)]` or `mod tests` block, and `grep -rn 'unwrap()' src/` is pasted into REPORT.md showing only test lines",
    "the five call sites report a caught RuntimeError naming the clock rather than panicking, and a Redblue `try`/`catch error` around a failing `time.now()` yields the error instead of aborting the process",
    "the failure is exercised WITHOUT moving the machine clock: the seconds-since-epoch conversion is extracted behind a function that takes the instant as an argument, so a test passes a pre-epoch instant directly and asserts the error — the test is deterministic and does not depend on wall-clock state",
    "edge tests named edge_* cover a pre-epoch instant, the exact epoch boundary, and `time.format` given an argument that is not a number or not a text",
    "`cargo test --all-targets` reports 0 failures and the 39 tests in tests/stdlib_modules_test.rs, including `network_timeouts_are_published_and_bounded`, still pass",
    "`cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` is clean with no `allow(` added"
